#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100509 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef779b81-341a-59eb-8bd4-860d5d58507c" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity"}] :question/id #uuid "17b77faa-ab56-5302-bc25-3cc7152243c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-13020" :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^6$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.15)^6$. Give your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer,\n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 + x)^{6} &= 1 + 6x + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot x^{2} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 4 \\cdot x^{3} + ⋯ + x^{n} \\\\\n&= 1 + 6x + 15x^{2} + 20x^{3} + ⋯ + x^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left({1 + x} \\right)^6 \\approx 1 + 6x + 15x^2 + 20x^3\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0.15$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1.15)^{6} &= (1 + 0.15)^{6} \\\\\n&\\approx 1 + 6(0.15)^{1} + 15(0.15)^{2} + 20(0.15)^{3} \\\\\n&= 1 + 6(0.15) + 15(0.0225) + 20(0.003375) \\\\\n&= 1 + 0.9 + 0.3375 + 0.0675 \\\\\n&= 2.305\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100510 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e430ab-a814-5613-a45d-d8cdd3c14a0f" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Close to Unity: Case With Alternating Signs"}] :question/id #uuid "c284dfb0-75ca-5f48-b44b-c2ce5ea37d4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-13021" :question/problem "Use the first four terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^8$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(0.89)^8$. Give your answer to six decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer,\n\n$$\n(1+x)^n = 1 + nx + \\dfrac{1}{2!} n(n-1)x^2 + \\dfrac{1}{3!} n(n-1)(n-2)x^3 + \\cdots + x^n\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 + x)^{8} &= 1 + 8x + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot x^{2} + \\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot x^{3} + ⋯ + x^{n} \\\\\n&= 1 + 8x + 28x^{2} + 56x^{3} + ⋯ + x^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left({1 + x} \\right)^8 \\approx 1 + 8x + 28x^2 + 56x^3\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.11$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^8{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(0.89)^{8} &= (1 + (-0.11))^{8} \\\\\n&\\approx 1 + 8(-0.11)^{1} + 28(-0.11)^{2} + 56(-0.11)^{3} \\\\\n&= 1 - 8(0.11) + 28(0.0121) - 56(0.001331) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.88 + 0.3388 - 0.074536 \\\\\n&= 0.384264\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto six decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100511 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4395c989-325f-5ac7-bdb3-f577f18695ed" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating Powers of Numbers Using the Binomial Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "f8a06578-35af-5510-90ca-8ca2f8c8203f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13022" :question/problem "Use the first $3$ terms of the binomial expansion of ${\\left(2+x \\right)^{4}}$ in ascending powers of $x$ to approximate $(1.97)^{4}$." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a=2$, $b=x$, and $n=4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n(2 + x)^{4} = \\mid (\\frac{4}{0})(2)^{4}(x)^{0} + (\\frac{4}{1})(2)^{4 - 1}(x)^{1} + (\\frac{4}{2})(2)^{4 - 2}(x)^{2} + ⋯; = 1 \\cdot (2)^{4}(x)^{0} + 4(2)^{3}(x)^{1} + 6(2)^{2}(x)^{2} + ⋯; = 16 + 4(8)(x) + 6(4)(x^{2}) + ⋯; = 16 + 32x + 24x^{2} + ⋯\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\left({2 + x} \\right)^{4} \\approx 16 + 32x + 24{x^2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-0.03$ into the expansion of $(2+x)^4{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1.97)^{4} &= (2 - 0.03)^{4} \\\\\n&\\approx 16 + 32(-0.03) + 24(-0.03)^{2} \\\\\n&= 16 - 0.96 + 0.0216 \\\\\n&= 15.0616\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100512 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e74e4a-8641-54c9-9d9d-c3ca53c94f8f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Percentage Error of an Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "461efa2a-0fd5-5a28-843d-422a56fbf031" :question/math-academy-id "e-13023" :question/problem "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.08)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^5$ in ascending powers of $x$. Round your final percentage error to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem for positive integer $n$ states that\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\ldots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nBy substituting $a = 1$, $b = x$, and $n = 5$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 + x)^{5} &= (\\frac{5}{0})(1)^{5}(x)^{0} + (\\frac{5}{1})(1)^{5 - 1}(x)^{1} + (\\frac{5}{2})(1)^{5 - 2}(x)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1(1)^{5} + 5(1)^{4}(x)^{1} + 10(1)^{3}(x)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + 5(1)(x) + 10(1)(x^{2}) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1 + 5x + 10x^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\left({1 + x} \\right)^{5}} \\approx 1 + 5x + 10x^2\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x = 0.08$ into the above approximation of $(1 + x)^5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1.08)^{5} &= (1 + 0.08)^{5} \\\\\n&\\approx 1 + 5(0.08) + 10(0.08)^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 + 5(0.08) + 10(0.0064) \\\\\n&= 1 + 0.4 + 0.064 \\\\\n&= 1.464\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n%error &= \\frac{amount of error}{exact value} \\cdot 100 \\\\\n&= (\\mid (1.08)^{5} - 1.464 \\mid)/((1.08)^{5}) \\cdot 100 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.36%\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowled